<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Andalus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Andalus was the name given to the parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> under Muslim rule from 711 until 1492. It was conquered under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and later ruled by an independent Umayyad dynasty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, outside Abbasid control. In the eleventh and twelfth centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> intervened from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> to rule it and defend it against the Christian kingdoms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-andalus.jpg" alt="al-Andalus" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Q4767211492 rowanwindwhistler · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Andalus732.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shi'a supporters who had expected a dynasty descended from Ali felt betrayed, the Umayyad survivor Abd al-Rahman I escaped to establish a rival emirate in <b>al-Andalus</b>, and Abu Muslim himself remained a dangerous independent power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its founder, Muhammad ibn Tumart, was a Berber theologian of the Masmuda who had studied in <b>al-Andalus</b> and the Islamic East.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almoravid economy rested on three things: control of the trans-Saharan gold trade, the agricultural revenues of Morocco and western Algeria, and the urban economy of <b>al-Andalus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Caliphate of Córdoba was proclaimed by Abd al-Rahman III, the eighth Umayyad ruler of <b>al-Andalus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The County of Toulouse grew out of the Carolingian reorganisation of the south-west after Charlemagne’s campaigns in Aquitaine and against <b>al-Andalus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ferdinand turned the combined resources against <b>al-Andalus</b>, which had fragmented after the Almohad defeat at the Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
